FULL BODY (verbatim, tests/npcSlice3a.test.ts:339-352):
```
  it('the cooldown is TICKED BEFORE it is CHECKED, so the last frame of it is already spent (`:163` then `:197`)', () => {
    const swing = (cooldown: number): number => {
      const a = bear({ target: new Dummy(511) }).npc;
      a.destX = 9999;
      a.startAction(OG_STATE.RUN);
      a.idleCounter = cooldown;
      a.doAction();
      return a.actionState;
    };
    // 2 → ticked to 1 → `1 <= 0` is false → no swing.
    expect(swing(2)).toBe(OG_STATE.RUN);
    // 1 → ticked to 0 → `0 <= 0` is TRUE → it swings on the same frame.
    expect(swing(1)).toBe(OG_STATE.ATTACK);
  });
```
THE TRAP LINE: `a.idleCounter = cooldown;` (one line before `a.doAction()`). The mechanism, read from the port: src/core/npc/EnemyBear.ts `doAction()` line 160 is `if (this.idleCounter > ZERO) this.idleCounter -= ONE;` — BEFORE the `switch (this.actionState)` — and the RUN case at line 182 then reads it: `if (this.idleCounter <= ZERO && target !== null && Math.abs(this.x - target.x) < OG_BEAR.attack.rangeX)`. So a setup value of 1 is decremented to 0 by the same `doAction()` call that tests it, and the bear swings. The neighbouring test (tests/npcSlice3a.test.ts:318-337, 'mauls at 99 px and not at 100') uses `idleCounter = 0`, which is why it was unaffected.
NOT VERIFIABLE: the ORIGINAL failing text was never committed (`git log --oneline --all -- tests/npcSlice3a.test.ts` returns only aa4f345 and the two 3b commits), so the pre-re-authoring source line is UNVERIFIED. From the log's wording ("a cooldown of 1 is already spent that frame. Re-authored as a two-sided boundary (2 -> no swing, 1 -> swings)") the original almost certainly read `a.idleCounter = 1;` and asserted `expect(a.actionState).toBe(OG_STATE.RUN)` — i.e. the failure message was the opaque `expected 10 to be 1` (ATTACK=10, RUN=1).

FULL BODY (verbatim, tests/npcSlice3b.test.ts:530-546):
```
  it('the pounce-mid clip ending has NO `actionState !== FALL` guard; the wolf\'s does (`:190-192`)', () => {
    // Airborne on purpose: on the ground, FALL lands on the first frame and the
    // LAND clip would mask what is being tested.
    const a = alpha().npc;
    a.setPosition(400, GROUND - 200);
    a.startAction(OG_STATE.FALL);
    a.startAnim(OG_ANIM.WOLF_POUNCE_MID);
    for (let i = 0; i < CLIP; i++) a.update();
    expect(a.getAnim()).toBe(OG_ANIM.WOLF_POUNCE_END);

    const w = wolf().npc;
    w.setPosition(400, GROUND - 200);
    w.startAction(OG_STATE.FALL);
    w.startAnim(OG_ANIM.WOLF_POUNCE_MID);
    for (let i = 0; i < CLIP; i++) w.update();
    expect(w.getAnim()).toBe(OG_ANIM.WOLF_POUNCE_MID); // its guard refuses to advance
  });
```
THE TRAP: without the `setPosition(400, GROUND - 200)` line the actor sits at y = GROUND, because the shared `stats()` factory hook does `npc.setPosition(400, GROUND)` (tests/npcSlice3b.test.ts:100, GROUND = 358 = world.groundY). Then, inside the FIRST `a.update()`: src/core/npc/EnemyWolfAlpha.ts `update()` calls `stepAnimation()` (POUNCE_MID goes to frame 1 of 3, returns '') and then `doAction()`, whose FALL case (lines 366-373) is `this.y += this.velY; this.velY += OG_NPC_LAUNCH.gravity; if (this.y >= this.world.groundY) { this.y = this.world.groundY; this.startAction(OG_STATE.LAND); }` — velY is 0, 358 >= 358, so on frame 1 the actor leaves FALL for LAND and `startAction(LAND)` runs `this.startAnim(OG_ANIM.WOLF_LAUNCHEDEND)` (line 262-263), destroying the POUNCE_MID clip under test. With a 3-frame clip (CLIP = 3) the LAUNCHEDEND clip only gets 2 steps by the assertion, so `getAnim()` returns WOLF_LAUNCHEDEND, not WOLF_POUNCE_END — exactly the log's "the LAND clip masked what was under test".
THIS IS THE ONLY ONE OF THE THREE that matches HARNESS_KICKOFF §T5's stated root.

FULL BODY (verbatim, tests/npcSlice3b.test.ts:615-623):
```
  it('it reads no cutscene at all, where every other animal has exactly one (`grep`: 0)', () => {
    // rng 0.5 → int(1.5) === 1: not 0, so no howl; and 1 < 2, so the walk roll passes.
    // Every other animal's idle re-roll is fenced by `!isShowing()`; the alpha's is not.
    const a = alpha({ rng: seq(0.5), world: { cutsceneShowing: true } }).npc;
    a.startAction(OG_STATE.IDLE);
    a.idleCounter = 1;
    a.doAction();
    expect(a.actionState).toBe(OG_STATE.WALK); // it walked anyway, mid-cutscene
  });
```
WHY 0.9 FAILED, from the source: ONE rng value has to satisfy TWO different bands.
  roll A (the howl escape), src/core/npc/EnemyWolfAlpha.ts:205 — `if (!this.world.adventureMode && asInt(this.rng() * OG_ALPHA.howlDice) === ZERO)`; OG_ALPHA.howlDice = 3 (src/config/ogEnemyWolfAlpha.ts:43). 0.9 → asInt(2.7) = 2, not 0 → no howl. PASSES.
  roll B (the walk escape), src/core/npc/EnemyWolfAlpha.ts:332 — `if (asInt(this.rng() * OG_ALPHA.idle.walkDice) < OG_ALPHA.idle.walkBelow) this.startAction(OG_STATE.WALK);`; `idle: { snarlFrames: 24, retryFrames: 12, walkDice: 3, walkBelow: 2 }` (src/config/ogEnemyWolfAlpha.ts:46). 0.9 → asInt(2.7) = 2, and `2 < 2` is FALSE → NO WALK. FAILS.
  0.5 satisfies both: asInt(1.5) = 1, which is not 0 (no howl) and is < 2 (walks).
WHY 0.9 WAS REACHED FOR: it is this suite's idiomatic "no howl" value — tests/npcSlice3b.test.ts:373 `const a = new EnemyWolfAlpha(world(), anims, geometry, seq(0.9), null); // 0.9 → no howl` and :398 `const right = alpha({ rng: seq(0.9) }).npc;`. The habit is documented in the file itself.
THE FAILURE MESSAGE was `expected 0 to be 2` (IDLE = 0, WALK = 2) — no mention of rng at all.

HOW A TEST SUPPLIES A SEQ TODAY: only through the constructor, via the factory's `Opts.rng` — `const npc = new EnemyBoar(world({...}), anims, geometry, o.rng ?? seq(0.5), t, o.doChase ?? false);` (tests/npcSlice3b.test.ts:112-119). Call sites look like `alpha({ rng: seq(0.5) })`, `boar({ rng: counting(0) })`, or raw `new EnemyWolfAlpha(world(), anims, geometry, seq(0.9), null)`.
CONSEQUENCE FOR rollsFor(npc, fn): it cannot legally reassign `npc.rng` — the field is `protected readonly`, so TypeScript rejects it from a test file and only an `as unknown as { rng: Rng }` cast gets through (runtime assignment DOES work: the field is a plain own property, `useDefineForClassFields: true`, nothing frozen). Cast precedent exists in tests (tests/AISeamController.test.ts:194 `(ai as unknown as Record<string, unknown>).react`, tests/combo.test.ts:63), but `strictTypeChecked` will police it.
THE CLEANER ROUTE, already the suites' own idiom: construct with `counting()` and measure a DELTA. This is proto-rollsFor and it already ships — tests/npcSlice3b.test.ts:292-301:
```
    const room = counting(0);
    const a = boar({ rng: room, target: new Dummy(9999) }).npc;
    a.startAction(OG_STATE.RUN); // consumes the knocker roll
    const before = room.calls;
    a.destX = a.posX; // arrived
    a.walkSpeed = 0;
    a.doAction();
    expect(a.actionState).toBe(OG_STATE.WALK);
    // the WALK it entered rolls a destX; the arrival COIN is never rolled.
    expect(room.calls - before).toBe(1);
```
and tests/npcSlice3a.test.ts:283-289 (`const rng = counting(0.4); ... expect(rng.calls).toBe(1);`) and :222-227 (`expect(rng.calls, name).toBe(2);`). So `rollsFor(npc, fn)` can be implemented with ZERO behaviour change as: require the npc to have been built with a `counting()` rng (pass it in, or accept `{npc, rng}`), snapshot `.calls`, run `fn()`, return the delta — no cast, no reassignment.

